    "cross": "Cross-Impact and Post-Treatment Durability",
    "bulk": "Control Points When Moving from Trial to Production",
    "generic1": "When developing a process, record the fibre composition and base construction, fabric mass, pre-treatment shrinkage, ground shade, dyeing/printing history, moisture and surface condition. For each operation, distinguish machine setpoints from the actual result on the fabric. Record actual temperature, dwell time, speed, tension, chemical add-on or coating level using an appropriate measurement method. Do not transfer settings from one fibre or construction to another without validation.",
    "generic2": "A treatment may improve one property while reducing another. Check appearance, shade variation, colourfastness, tensile/tear strength where relevant, shrinkage, wetting or water resistance, air permeability, coating adhesion and hand feel together. Trials should include an untreated control, a post-treatment sample and a sample after washing/ageing according to intended use. Record the test method and conditioning so results can be compared.",
    "generic3": "Before bulk production, confirm the recipe, operation sequence, repeatability between batches, uniformity across fabric width and roll length, drying/setting conditions, sampling method and acceptance limits agreed with the customer. When a deviation occurs, isolate it by raw-material lot, machine, shift, recipe and time of occurrence. Avoid changing several variables at once, as this makes root-cause analysis difficult. Retain reference samples, process sheets, test results and change history.",
    "noticeTitle": "Safety and Compliance Note",
    "noticeText": "Finishing chemicals, printing inks, ozone, lasers, heat presses and embroidery machines each present specific risks. Operate only in accordance with the SDS/TDS, equipment instructions, risk assessment and local regulations. Antimicrobial, UV-protective, flame-retardant or waterproof claims require testing and legal review within the correct scope.",
